{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-165777",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $A$ represent the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to pick $3$ jelly beans out of $25,$ and $n$ is the number of ways to choose exactly $2$ green and $1$ red.\n\nSince the order in which Ted picks the jelly beans does not matter, we use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N.$ The number of ways to pick $3$ jelly beans from a total of $25$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N={}_{25}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{25!}{22!\\cdot 3!}=2\\,300.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n.$ The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps: \n\n- the number of ways to choose $2$ (out of $10$) green jelly beans is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{2}=\\frac{10!}{8!\\cdot 2!}=45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the number of ways to choose $1$ (out of $15$) red jelly beans is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{15}{C}_{1} & =\\frac{15!}{14!\\cdot 1!}=15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n={}_{10}{C}_{2}\\cdot {}_{15}{C}_{1}=45\\cdot 15=675.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{675}{2300}=\\frac{27}{92}≈0.29=29%.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
